{
  "format": "architecture-publisher.model",
  "version": 3,
  "id": "ddwg",
  "locale": "en",
  "title": "Data Department Work Guidelines",
  "question": "How do the Data Department work guidelines lead a reader to the rule that owns a task, and how is a change to them proposed, checked and released?",
  "summary": "One task map routes each work question to one of seven topics under the charter. Six OpenSpec capability specs state what the guidance and its upkeep must do, installed ETHOS admits and proves every change, and each Forge receives the signed edition on its own.",
  "scope": "The guidelines as released at v8.0.0: the entries, the task map, the seven topics, the decision records, the maintenance documents, the OpenSpec workspace with its six capability specs, and the ETHOS, tool, check and CI configuration that keeps them.",
  "limits": [
    "A relation records a link, a named path or a requirement by which one component routes a reader to another, places a duty with it, constrains it, or generates, installs or runs it; it cites the files that make that reference.",
    "Links between the seven topics, links back to an entry, the task map or the governance page, and pointers that another document already holds are listed in the notes rather than drawn.",
    "The archived Change history is outside the scope; documents and checks are described, not their observed use by the department."
  ],
